WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:06.930
- Welcome, thank you so much

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.790
for joining us for Aid,
Geopolitics and Power,

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.690
our human rights back on
international aid agenda.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.250
This is the fifth in a series

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.740
the Yale Development Dialogues
Economic Policy Lessons

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.570
from History, which is co-hosted

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:19.869
by the Yale Economic Growth Center.

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:21.710
The South Asian Studies Council

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.110
from the Yale MacMillan Center

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.430
and the Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:28.120
I'm Catherine Cheney, and
I'll be your moderator today.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.460
I'll briefly introduce the topic

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.011
and our panelists before we dive in.

00:00:31.011 --> 00:00:33.020
But as I always reiterate,

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.220
these events are designed
to be conversational

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.040
and we really value your questions.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.390
So if you're joining us on Zoom

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.050
please keep your questions
coming throughout in the Q and A

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.170
and we'll get to them as soon as we can.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.150
So this topic is such a great fit

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.840
for our approach in this series,

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.950
Bringing Historians and
Practitioners Together.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.750
When higher income countries get involved

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.310
in lower income countries,
whether through foreign aid

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.950
disaster relief or military intervention

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.220
they can justify their acts

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.690
by the goals to reduce
poverty, protect human rights

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.630
or advance their own interests.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.980
And part of why we're having
this conversation today is due

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.860
to some recent structural changes

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.830
at major international
development agencies,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.020
here in the US as well as in the UK,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.810
marking shift in priorities.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.410
So, yesterday was Samantha
Power's confirmation hearing

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.326
as president Joe Biden's
pick for administrator

00:01:18.326 --> 00:01:21.230
for the US agency for
international development.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:23.193
And as many of you know

00:01:23.193 --> 00:01:25.010
the UK is department for
international development merge

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.410
with the foreign Commonwealth office

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.218
uniting development and
diplomacy in one new department

00:01:29.218 --> 00:01:32.770
the Foreign Commonwealth and
Development Office, or FCDO.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.790
So USAID and FCDO are two acronyms

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:36.350
you'll be hearing about a lot today

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.660
as some of the shifts we're seeing.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.250
And we'll talk about what
lessons history might offer.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.870
What lessons might history offer

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.430
as these agencies changed their structure

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.030
in pursuit of new priorities.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.240
Our panelists are gonna look
at foreign interventions

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.960
of the past, their stated
intentions and real-world effects.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.990
And they'll ask what this renewed emphasis

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.370
on human rights might mean

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.967
for poverty reduction in a
world of extreme inequality.

00:01:57.967 --> 00:02:00.079
So joining us to discuss these topics

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:02.207
I'd like to introduce Melissa Dell.

00:02:02.207 --> 00:02:05.490
She's a professor of economics
at Harvard University.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.090
Her work focuses on long
run economic development

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.270
primarily in Latin America and Asia.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.840
And she's conducted research
on how US military strategies
in Vietnam had a lasting impact

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.370
on that country's institutions.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.357
You'll hear more about that

00:02:17.357 --> 00:02:19.800
and some of her other research today.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.910
Samuel Moyn is Henry R. Luce
professor of Jurisprudence

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.810
at Yale Law School and
professor of history

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.230
at Yale University and author
of "Not Enough Human Rights

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.127
In An Unequal World," very
relevant to today's discussion.

00:02:31.127 --> 00:02:32.670
His areas of interest

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.880
in legal scholarship
include international law,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.390
human rights, the law of war

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.210
and legal thought in both historical

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.760
and current perspective.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.810
And two familiar names for
those of you who have joined us

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.227
before Rohini Pande is the Henry
J Heinz II Professor of Economics

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.040
and director of the economic
growth center at Yale.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.330
Her research is largely
focused on how formal

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.599
and informal institutions
shape power relationships

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:57.599
and patterns of economic and
political advantage in society

00:02:57.599 --> 00:03:00.760
particularly in developing countries.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.470
And Rory Stewart is a senior fellow

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.970
at the Yale Jackson
Institute for Global Affairs

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.160
where he focuses on contemporary politics

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.960
in crisis and on international
development and intervention

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.880
in fragile and conflict affected States.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.980
And Rory also served as the UK secretary

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.520
of state for international
development, DFID

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:18.840
which I mentioned earlier

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.520
where he doubled the UK investment

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.740
in international climate and environment.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.990
So I'd like to start with Rohini actually

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:29.700
because Rohini was really
the person who put this

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.950
on the agenda for our
Yale Development Dialogues

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.550
as something to discuss, something

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:37.130
where history may offer lessons for today.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.610
So Rohini, can you expand

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:39.810
on some of the trends you're seeing

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.570
in foreign aid development policy

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.580
and governance and the 21st century

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.300
particularly in a low and
middle income country context

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.590
and what does this demand,
or why does this demand

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:51.280
this question we're asking
today, are human rights back

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.880
on the international aid agenda?

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:55.190
- Thanks Catherine.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.570
And thanks everyone for joining.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.360
So let me just step back a couple

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.140
of decades to the start
of the 21st century.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.480
I think it was a very heady time

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.240
for international development.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.340
Economic growth in many parts of Asia

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.710
were fast reducing poverty,
and coming on the heels

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.631
of the Jubilee 2000 debt
relief, rich countries

00:04:13.631 --> 00:04:17.280
were typically expanding
the aid programs so much

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.591
so that according to one
Brookings estimate by 2006

00:04:19.591 --> 00:04:21.882
the amount of foreign aid available

00:04:21.882 --> 00:04:25.270
in the world was sufficient
to bring every extreme

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.621
poor person up to the
threshold of a dollar 90 a day.

00:04:28.621 --> 00:04:32.310
And alongside on the research
side in development economics

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:33.610
we saw a growing interest

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.310
in quantifying the impact
of development policies.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.161
And this was exemplified by the rise

00:04:38.161 --> 00:04:40.871
of the experimental analysis
of development projects.

00:04:40.871 --> 00:04:43.975
But now the research has
flourished and was exemplified

00:04:43.975 --> 00:04:48.975
by the Nobel prize in 2019 to
Banerjee, Duflo and Kremer.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.661
I think the politics of aid
started taking a different turn

00:04:51.661 --> 00:04:54.523
in the second decade of the 21st century.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.770
So in 2013, both Canada

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.230
and Australia merged their aid departments

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:01.990
with their foreign offices.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.500
And then Catherine, as you
mentioned in June of last year

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.360
Boris Johnson announced the merger

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.170
of the foreign Commonwealth
office with the Department

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.980
of International Development
to create what they described

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.460
as a new super department,

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.160
the Foreign Commonwealth
and Development Office.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:18.993
And most recently

00:05:18.993 --> 00:05:22.040
Samantha Power has been
formally given a table

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.402
at the National Security Council,

00:05:23.402 --> 00:05:25.741
recognizing that the mission of USAID

00:05:25.741 --> 00:05:28.530
alongside defense and
diplomacy is contributing

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:29.683
to national security.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:32.270
And the common narrative

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.450
which actually goes
back to what Albert said

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:39.070
as Australian head at the time
of the merger was like, good

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.910
we're going to bring it
back inside the department

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.050
because you want
Australia's aid program to

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.480
be fully integrated into our
overall diplomatic effort.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:48.990
We don't want a diplomacy going

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.450
in one direction and our aid
program going in another.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:53.670
And so what we see today

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.510
is that rich countries
are increasingly explicit

00:05:56.510 --> 00:06:00.270
about a desire to leverage
foreign aid for domestic goals.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.560
And I think this raises this question

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.853
of what happens to the
world's poor and vulnerable?

00:06:03.853 --> 00:06:07.670
And so this brings us today to
the start of the third decade

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:11.333
of the 21st century, which I
see as marked by two forces.

00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:14.330
First, we're entering
a decade where perhaps

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.960
because of austerity
policies in rich countries

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.420
because of growing
inequality, rich countries

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:22.970
are extremely willing to see
development quite explicitly

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.290
as a situation of par, one way

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:26.870
they're going to use the resources

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:30.720
and the geopolitical
might to buy leverage.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.810
But importantly, we are
also entering a decade

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:35.920
of democratic backsliding
in many parts of the world.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.991
So we are seeing cases of elected rulers

00:06:38.991 --> 00:06:42.120
weakening checks, and
executive park, tailing

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.050
political and civil liberties,
and undermining the integrity

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:46.762
of the electoral system very often.

00:06:46.762 --> 00:06:50.353
And I'd say lower and higher
middle income countries.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:52.560
And this is what really brings me

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.231
to the idea that I think we
need revisit the implications

00:06:55.231 --> 00:06:58.490
of putting human rights on the table.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:01.600
To date very few aid
agencies have led with it

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.719
but as we see growing
inequality, which makes it harder

00:07:04.719 --> 00:07:08.950
for them to doll out just
cash transfers to the poor.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:12.150
And as we see democratic
backsliding, we want

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.580
I think it's important to
ask what the challenges are

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.920
in putting human rights

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.593
as being central to development agenda.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.040
Let me just say what I see

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.889
as the challenge for development
economists, which I am one.

00:07:24.889 --> 00:07:28.730
I think that the challenges
that over the last two decades

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.690
we've seen a lot of
push on testing programs

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:32.760
in the social sector.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.268
Of saying we want to put
resources where we can test it,

00:07:37.268 --> 00:07:39.948
but how do you do, how
do you actually identify

00:07:39.948 --> 00:07:43.520
how can you help liberal democracies

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.200
in the developing world?

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.330
You know, what institutions
can we support?

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.970
How do we actually test
whether that works?

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:50.870
And then, you know

00:07:52.071 --> 00:07:54.690
how do we persuade rich
countries to put money there?

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.710
But instance, one example
where I think we do

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.880
have evidence is on
independent and credible media.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.200
We have a lot of evidence

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.320
from actually field experiments suggesting

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.350
that they're held with accountability.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:08.330
But to me, the open question
is how is this actually

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.890
going to become part of
what aid agencies do?

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:14.540
So over to Catherine with that.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:15.550
- Thank you, Rohini.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:18.240
And you set us up well by talking about

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.340
how rising inequality
may demand some change,

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:25.120
may put human rights back on the table

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.128
even if agencies haven't led with it.

00:08:27.128 --> 00:08:29.740
And that brings me to a question for Sam.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:32.240
So Sam, in your book,
you explore why the rise

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.950
of human rights over time
has not actually resulted

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.440
in social and economic
justice, but instead happened

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.120
alongside growing inequality
as Rohini described.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.333
So can you expand on
that disconnect for us?

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.300
- Sure, to begin with,
thanks for the invitation.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:50.600
And thanks for having me, I
look forward to learning a lot

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:55.270
you know, I'm, I would have said

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:57.189
that starting in the forties

00:08:57.189 --> 00:09:00.580
the human rights agenda
and the development agenda

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:03.960
have been pretty disconnected
and remain so to this day,

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:04.920
it's really never

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.730
the twain shall meet rather than

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:10.750
the idea that human rights
were connected to development

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.333
or are now being put back.

00:09:14.237 --> 00:09:17.680
In the 1940s, there was
the universal declaration

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.466
of human rights which came along

00:09:19.466 --> 00:09:23.420
with some basic standards
in the economic realm

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.987
which we call economic and social rights.

00:09:25.987 --> 00:09:30.987
Harry Truman in my country announced 0.4

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.930
which is conventionally dated

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:37.150
as the kind of origin of
development assistance.

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:42.150
The trouble is that the
ideologies of development

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:46.070
have, you know waxed and waned according

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:50.239
to different orthodoxies
amongst economists

00:09:50.239 --> 00:09:53.440
and have been pretty tightly linked

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.400
to national security
agendas of giving states.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.260
And that is likely to remain true

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.350
for as long as we can imagine.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.170
And as far as I can tell,

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.830
there really hasn't been much connection.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:09.952
Even once human rights began
to be taken more seriously

00:10:09.952 --> 00:10:13.320
by the United States
and the United Kingdom

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.510
as foreign policy aspirations in the 1970s

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:18.343
there are a couple

00:10:18.343 --> 00:10:20.440
of sets of exceptions
to that generalization.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:23.740
There was conditionality, saying

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:28.568
that foreign aid had to be
kind of used as a carrot

00:10:28.568 --> 00:10:33.289
to in incentivize bad regimes to treat

00:10:33.289 --> 00:10:35.750
their peoples less badly.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:40.750
There was also some, some
intersection between Jimmy Carter

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.810
and that president who kind
of brought human rights

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:49.130
to the global stage and
his development people

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:53.072
who began to talk a lot about basic needs.

00:10:53.072 --> 00:10:58.072
You know, to come to your
question, my own, you know, thesis

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:03.600
has been that human rights name
a kind of sufficiency idea.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.940
The basic entitlements in
that every human should get

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:13.000
on the basis of his or their humanity

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:15.300
you know, housing, food, health.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.210
That is not the same

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:20.290
as a campaign against global poverty.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:24.080
Although it's true that the
campaign against global poverty

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.301
of the last 40 or 50 years
has also been about a very

00:11:27.301 --> 00:11:31.350
a flow of sufficient provision.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:32.310
It's much lower.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.920
Let's be honest, a
dollar and change a day.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:38.190
The world bank standard
of express extreme poverty

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:39.712
that Rohini mentioned

00:11:39.712 --> 00:11:44.150
is not the schedule of
economic and social rights.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:46.970
More to the point human rights, activists

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:50.440
even once they began taking economic

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.670
and social rights more
seriously alongside free speech

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.118
and bodily integrity and things like that,

00:11:56.118 --> 00:12:01.118
have been persistently
critical of development theory

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:06.300
for neglecting the much
higher floor of protection

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.970
that human rights are supposed to promise.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:13.970
As of today I would say there's,
I wouldn't think of aside

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.393
from Samantha Powers appointment
and elevation that there's

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:22.850
there's a lot of prospect

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:26.090
for human rights coming to development.

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:27.501
I mean, that could be a rhetoric.

00:12:27.501 --> 00:12:32.250
What it means is hard to say

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:37.023
and not on its power is not
even on the USAID website yet.

00:12:38.112 --> 00:12:42.918
For one thing, the eradication

00:12:42.918 --> 00:12:44.820
of poverty has mostly not worked

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:51.000
through any kind of human
rights agenda to date.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.850
And it's been very spotty,
highly localized in,

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:56.900
on the surface of the world.

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:59.690
We've just lived through
a pandemic, you know

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:01.410
we're in the final stages of it

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:04.850
hopefully at least in the global
North, which has seen a lot

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:09.340
of reversals in the anti poverty agenda.

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:14.340
I would close by saying
it though, power is there.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:18.630
The stated foreign policy
agenda of Joe Biden

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:21.110
and his people is actually to care most

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:23.780
about the American middle class.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:26.740
And you see that I think most
graphically in a March, 3

00:13:27.951 --> 00:13:30.740
speech that new secretary
of state Antony Blinken gave

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:32.812
in which he says that every little bit

00:13:32.812 --> 00:13:36.290
of the reorientation of
American foreign policy

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:39.362
which must include what Samantha
Power is going to be doing

00:13:39.362 --> 00:13:44.362
is really focused on making
sure that policymakers

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:48.472
don't forget Northern working classes

00:13:48.472 --> 00:13:53.330
which led to Donald Trump,
you know, many think.

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:57.411
And so the, let's make
sure not to, you know,

00:13:57.411 --> 00:14:01.340
get fooled by the hype

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:04.523
that we're on the brink of vast changes.

00:14:09.746 --> 00:14:10.579
- Do you wanna close at that, all right

00:14:10.579 --> 00:14:12.690
we'll make sure not to
get to get into the hype

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:13.955
or at least we'll break down

00:14:13.955 --> 00:14:16.755
whether this is rhetoric
or whether this is real.

00:14:16.755 --> 00:14:21.070
And I took note of something Sam said

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.360
which I think really captures one

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:25.115
of the themes we're getting at here.

00:14:25.115 --> 00:14:28.910
That a flow of sufficient
provision does not

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:30.670
equal social and economic rights.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:32.880
And that development
theory tends to neglect

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:33.762
the much higher floor

00:14:33.762 --> 00:14:37.010
of protecting that human
rights are meant to promise.

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.880
And so we'll see if there's change

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:42.800
but Sam seems unconvinced

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:44.330
that we're on the brink of major change.

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:45.950
And I think that's great.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:47.550
We're gonna get into perhaps some debate

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.200
among our panelists a bit later.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.700
But I wanna go ahead and bring Melissa

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:53.050
into this conversation.

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:54.690
Melissa, your research has focused

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.080
on how foreign intervention
affects development

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.830
across the range of geographies.

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:00.100
And I'd love for you to expand

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.380
on some of your research in Vietnam.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.840
Those who may have seen
the promotional video

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:07.210
for this webinar might
have a preview of that.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:09.190
Your research in Vietnam,
as well as Mexico

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:13.080
and what lessons from those
experiences seem most urgent

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:14.030
and relevant today?

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:16.870
- Yep, thank you so much.

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:21.870
And thank you also for
inviting me to be here.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:23.760
So I wanna start by picking up

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.410
on a point that came up before
when Rohini was talking,

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:32.180
which is this idea that
we've seen in recent years,

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:34.330
kind of geopolitical objectives

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.220
and development aid objectives

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:39.230
moving closer together and
becoming more intertwined.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:41.240
And I'd say that if anything

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:44.440
like that's been the
norm and maybe, you know

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:48.270
like in the period after
the cold war, you know

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:50.240
people were a bit less anxious

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:53.210
about those geopolitical
objectives and that relaxed a bit.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:55.040
But I think that that's changing now

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:56.660
and kind of with the rise of China

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.910
and that it's not an accident
that this is happening again.

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:01.160
And so if you go back

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:05.010
and you think about the
policies in the 1960s

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.870
with John F. Kennedy, the
Alliance for progress, you know

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:11.422
his other kind of
development aid, priorities

00:16:11.422 --> 00:16:12.255
those were very closely linked

00:16:12.255 --> 00:16:14.730
to the fight against
communism into the cold war.

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:18.400
And I think that that's
just the norm and, you know,

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.110
to tie this back and to the question

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:25.020
and to my research and, you
know, so broadly speaking

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:29.470
we can think about kind of two
different schools of thought

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:33.900
about how the US and
other rich countries kind

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:37.430
of achieved their geopolitical objectives

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:40.317
in low-income countries.

00:16:40.317 --> 00:16:43.530
And so again, if we go
back to the cold war

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:44.600
there was this very kind

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:48.380
of prominent view popularized the most

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:50.900
by like Walt Ross out who was an economist

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.379
and also Lyndon Johnson's
national security advisor

00:16:53.379 --> 00:16:58.377
that you know, communism is
this disease of the transition

00:16:58.377 --> 00:17:02.798
from being an agricultural
to an industrialized society.

00:17:02.798 --> 00:17:07.798
And, you know, the US needed
to stop poor countries

00:17:09.198 --> 00:17:11.830
from succumbing on, you
know, to that disease.

00:17:11.830 --> 00:17:13.558
And there were two general ways to do it.

00:17:13.558 --> 00:17:16.070
And so what Ross Stout

00:17:16.070 --> 00:17:20.420
and what others advocated for
is you do that through force.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:21.520
You make people realize

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:23.567
that it's gonna be extremely costly

00:17:23.567 --> 00:17:28.567
if they go against, you
know, the non-communist state

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.619
in the context of the cold war.

00:17:30.619 --> 00:17:34.490
And the other view which
was actually much less sort

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:36.200
of prominent historically.

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:37.730
But some people did argue for it is

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:42.150
that you have to give
people meaningful incentives

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:45.760
you know, to support a
democracy or to support

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.814
a non communist state through,
you know, through the state

00:17:48.814 --> 00:17:52.230
providing them things that
are meaningful to their lives

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:55.723
you know, schools,
health clinics, security

00:17:55.723 --> 00:17:57.310
things like that.

00:17:57.310 --> 00:17:58.930
And so this is a question that I studied

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:01.835
in the context of the Vietnam war,

00:18:01.835 --> 00:18:04.780
where the objective of the
US and the Vietnam war.

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.240
It wasn't so much, you know

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.650
stamping out specific
and surgeons per se, but

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:12.184
it was creating a South
Vietnamese state that would stand

00:18:12.184 --> 00:18:16.730
as a bulwark against communism
after the US withdrew.

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:19.092
And to have that people
have to support the state.

00:18:19.092 --> 00:18:23.130
And so they're very explicitly
kind of social scientists,

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:25.150
economists, political
scientists, et cetera

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:27.800
they thought about like,
well, how do you get people

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:31.460
to support the state and
sort of the dominant view

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:34.410
and what the US overwhelmingly
pursued was to do that

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:36.250
through overwhelming firepower.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.405
You make it extremely costly

00:18:38.405 --> 00:18:41.064
for them to go against the state.

00:18:41.064 --> 00:18:43.515
And I think like very much a game theory

00:18:43.515 --> 00:18:46.984
was influential kind of in
how people thought about this,

00:18:46.984 --> 00:18:50.670
and just kind of the
general intuition that

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:53.170
if you go out and you'd
be really aggressive

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:54.420
that they're gonna back down.

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:58.340
And so, you know, the US
dropped many times more bombs

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:01.180
on South Vietnam than were
dropped in all of Asia

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:02.540
and all of Europe from world war II,

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:04.940
just an overwhelming amount of firepower.

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:06.120
And in our paper

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.125
we're able to exploit exogenous variation

00:19:08.125 --> 00:19:11.710
from an algorithm that
the US use to target

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:14.576
these bombings to show
that that policy backfired.

00:19:14.576 --> 00:19:19.010
That when they bombed
civilian areas that rather

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:22.240
than people saying, Oh, like,
I better do what the US wants.

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:24.490
And what's out the South
Vietnamese government wants

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:25.510
I better support this state.

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:27.030
And so they wouldn't join the vehicle

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:31.374
because they were angry,
you know, rightfully so.

00:19:31.374 --> 00:19:33.974
But that wasn't like, you know, that

00:19:33.974 --> 00:19:37.130
wasn't the only thing
the US did in Vietnam.

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:39.266
That was the dominant perspective.

00:19:39.266 --> 00:19:44.266
But an interesting context
that we also look at

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:47.870
is that the US Marines by and
large took a different kind

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:50.600
of perspective on fighting the war.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.560
And so the US Marines have this sort

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:56.660
of long history of being like
the US Imperial police force.

00:19:56.660 --> 00:20:00.060
So to say, like in the Caribbean,
and, you know, I'm sure

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:02.617
that they did lots of horrible
human rights abuses there

00:20:02.617 --> 00:20:04.660
but compare that to the army.

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:07.130
Who's kind of, you know,
founding motives were just

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:09.308
to line up and shoot as
many people as possible.

00:20:09.308 --> 00:20:11.170
It's like a different perspective.

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:13.020
They worked a lot more
closely with locals.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:14.420
They did development aid,

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:16.470
and that's what they pushed into Vietnam.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.420
And so we look at this area

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:19.966
that was commanded by the Marines

00:20:19.966 --> 00:20:22.868
versus very nearby places that
were commanded by the army

00:20:22.868 --> 00:20:25.737
the Marines built schools,
they built health clinics.

00:20:25.737 --> 00:20:28.560
They work closely with locals.

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.497
And in those areas you
see far yet, you know

00:20:30.497 --> 00:20:34.430
less attacks on the US, you see

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:36.790
people are South Vietnamese citizens,

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:38.880
and public opinion data
are more supportive

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:42.370
of all levels of South
Vietnamese government, you know,

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.390
and the US they actually see public goods

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:46.730
actually being provided, you know

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:49.610
so we can't say, well,
would it have been better

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.970
if the US just didn't get
into this conflict at all?

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:53.830
You know, which there's
a strong argument for

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:56.230
but like conditional
kind of on being there

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:57.330
and finding this conflict

00:20:57.330 --> 00:20:59.200
this kind of development
aid approach appears

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:02.830
to be much more effective on,

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:04.920
and then kind of to tie this
idea into the president.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:07.600
I don't think that this is
something that is limited

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:10.410
to the cold war that is limited

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:12.340
to foreign intervention, per se.

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.000
There's just this broad idea of like

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:15.890
you have places that are not really

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:19.806
within the control of the
state, and this is very common.

00:21:19.806 --> 00:21:22.330
Like, how do you bring
those places, you know

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:24.250
under the control of a state?

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:26.250
And this was also like what I looked

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:30.160
at in Mexico at the
war on drugs in Mexico.

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:33.610
And so this is not exactly
foreign intervention though.

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:36.160
Obviously foreign policy
is a big motivator

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:37.140
for the war on drugs.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:39.430
And that is kind of, that's another kind

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.760
of rich country policy that
has had enormous implications

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:44.686
on for the developing world.

00:21:44.686 --> 00:21:47.780
But I look there at, you know, crack down

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:49.980
on the drug trade and it shows
something that's, you know

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:53.900
a little bit reminiscent
of what we found in Vietnam

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:56.026
which is that the state comes in

00:21:56.026 --> 00:21:58.708
they cracked down on the drug
trade in a given location

00:21:58.708 --> 00:22:01.820
and that weakens the
incumbent and leads, you know

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:03.560
their rivals to come in and try to fight.

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:06.230
And since the state actually
has fairly limited capacity

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:08.140
it just unleashes these drug wars

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:10.290
and then people just
take the drugs around.

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:14.052
And so, you know, it's not
as if you know, this is

00:22:14.052 --> 00:22:16.790
I think the goal of Mexico
was to get the drugs

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:18.460
to like go through the Caribbean,

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:19.930
but in the end that didn't happen.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:20.840
They just took other routes

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:23.483
in Mexico and it kind of
unleash this violence.

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:26.300
And, you know, of course the state side

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:27.450
of it is not the only side.

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:31.590
We also show that when young
men lose their jobs due

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:33.390
to trade shocks from
China, they're also more

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.070
likely to go join drug gangs
and that creates violence.

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:38.750
And so there's an economic
component as well.

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:40.300
But in general, I think that it's kind

00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:42.722
of reminiscent of the
same debates that came up

00:22:42.722 --> 00:22:44.910
in the sixties and seventies, you know

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:48.870
so I was talking with, you
know, some officials in Mexico

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:51.530
and there was a group,
like an influential group

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:54.340
that was really heavily
advocating, like, okay

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:57.160
let's not just fight
this on, through sending

00:22:57.160 --> 00:22:59.980
in the military, like,
let's give development aid.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:01.160
We know in some of these places

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:03.010
there's not other job opportunities.

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:04.930
People don't trust the state

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:06.260
because they've had a bad experience.

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:08.510
Maybe the state can come
in and build infrastructure

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:10.642
and that's useful for creating jobs.

00:23:10.642 --> 00:23:13.047
And, you know, they
almost got that program

00:23:13.047 --> 00:23:14.910
and they almost got that
program off the ground

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:16.550
but then somebody else
came in and was like

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:19.110
wait a minute, like,
we're national security.

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:21.030
Like we don't do development aid that's

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:22.570
for the social development ministry.

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:23.403
Like, what do you mean?

00:23:23.403 --> 00:23:26.160
Like we go in and we show
them who's in charge.

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:28.900
And I think, you know, to tie
it back into the beginning

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:32.100
and about kind of the most
pressing lessons, you know,

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:33.670
at least you know, I'm not an expert

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:35.362
on international diplomacy

00:23:35.362 --> 00:23:37.640
but people were arguing
that we're kind of moving

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:42.640
into this phase of kind of,
you know, a new cold war

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:45.330
something like it with
China, where China is rising.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:48.200
You know, they're taking kind
of a very aggressive stance

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:49.788
on the global stage and, you know

00:23:49.788 --> 00:23:52.223
how's that gonna play out?

00:23:52.223 --> 00:23:57.223
And in the kind of linking this idea

00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:00.380
of like development aid with diplomacy

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:02.570
like the reality is that
diplomacy is just always

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:04.560
gonna be, you know, that like,

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:07.610
and like the security
interests are gonna be

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:09.640
like the number one objective.

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:12.660
But is there a way to do
that where, you know, even

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:15.667
if it's like grossly
inadequate development aid,

00:24:15.667 --> 00:24:18.400
that's you know, getting
people's income up a few cents

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:21.252
or something that's better
than going and like, you know

00:24:21.252 --> 00:24:26.252
dropping bombs on them or, you
know, encouraging, you know

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:27.180
a government to take

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:29.270
on like a violent conflict
against people, right?

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:31.180
And so I think

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:35.050
that it is actually a
potentially promising thing

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:37.080
that these objectives
kind of get integrated.

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:38.540
And how do we think about, you know

00:24:38.540 --> 00:24:40.630
our influence on the
global stage, not just

00:24:40.630 --> 00:24:44.202
through being the kind of
aggressive, the most aggressive

00:24:44.202 --> 00:24:49.202
you know, country around, but
also giving people, you know

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:53.740
incentives to want to support the US

00:24:53.740 --> 00:24:56.648
or to wanna support,
you know, our objectives

00:24:56.648 --> 00:25:01.033
through a development
aid and other policies.

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:04.300
- Thanks, Melissa, really
helpful examples in terms of

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:05.950
when we look at this connection

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:08.450
between development and
diplomacy and defense,

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:11.608
how has it worked use incentives
versus force over time?

00:25:11.608 --> 00:25:13.711
And I'm glad you mentioned China.

00:25:13.711 --> 00:25:16.900
I hope that's something we can return to

00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:19.670
when we all jump into
discussion in a little bit,

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:22.010
and we have some great
questions coming in.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:24.760
So just a reminder to those
of us, those of you joining us

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:26.590
on zoom, please keep
those questions coming.

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:28.250
I'm excited to get to some of these,

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:29.740
but I wanna bring Rory into this.

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:31.480
So Rory, we mentioned earlier, some

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:36.008
of the changes happening
with foreign aid in the UK,

00:25:36.008 --> 00:25:38.810
that aid agenda has undergone
quite a transformation

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.559
over the past year, and it's
still changing it seems.

00:25:41.559 --> 00:25:44.980
So I'd like to ask you what
does this mean for the UK's work

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:47.440
on human rights and how has that changed

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:50.439
as a priority from when
you led DFID to now?

00:25:50.439 --> 00:25:52.433
And what do you expect next?

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:55.690
Oh Rory, you're on mute.

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.030
- First thing, first thing to understand

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:02.830
is that we are going through in the UK

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:07.910
what is a pretty natural
evolution, I guess

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:10.750
for every country and has
some implications in US.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:13.620
Which is that DFID was set up

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.300
with a very idealistic structure.

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:17.010
So in the late 1990s

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:20.117
the idea was that UK
would commit to spend 0.7%

00:26:20.117 --> 00:26:23.100
of its GDP on international development.

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:25.740
And it would create this
completely independent apartment

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:28.060
of government cabinet level department

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:29.760
which would have basically nothing to do

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:33.270
with the foreign service
or the ministry of defense.

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:36.850
And it was set up by law that
it could only spend money

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:39.239
in order to tackle extreme politics.

00:26:39.239 --> 00:26:41.270
And this was deliberately done

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:42.970
because obviously the people working

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:45.917
for it and setting it up,
wanted to try to protect it

00:26:45.917 --> 00:26:48.580
against any of the other temptations.

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:50.410
So it was almost the purest possible kind

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:53.111
of incarnation of a dream of development.

00:26:53.111 --> 00:26:58.111
It was also set up so that there
could not be any confusions

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:00.890
with national interests
that were very, very

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:05.450
illegal actions to stop
people using this money

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:08.400
to try to procure British goods
to do anything of the sort.

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:13.450
From the beginning, this
was an enormous problem.

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:15.430
And a problem that the people working

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:19.760
for DFID probably did not
fully anticipate or understand.

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:21.480
They staff the department with many

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:24.380
many people who would found
anonymous or came out of NGOs.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:30.640
And they, at some level, people were very

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:33.210
very reluctant to get
involved in the conversation

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:35.210
with the public or other politicians

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:37.883
about what this money
was being used to do.

00:27:39.370 --> 00:27:41.290
And the scale of money you're talking to,

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:42.730
so by the time I was running DFID

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:46.060
we had a budget of about 20
billion US dollars a year

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:49.080
totally unrestricted, right.

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:50.550
I had no control at all

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:52.760
about really how that money was spent.

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:54.200
Because it was almost impossible

00:27:54.200 --> 00:27:56.130
for any other government
department to get its hands

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:57.370
on that money.

00:27:57.370 --> 00:28:00.150
In addition, austerity was taking place.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:02.090
So I remember this
extraordinary meeting that I had

00:28:02.090 --> 00:28:05.510
with Rohini when I just be moved from my

00:28:05.510 --> 00:28:07.420
I think my second place to DFID.

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:09.170
I've been moved to run UK prisons

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:11.320
before I went back to DFID again.

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:14.970
And I moved from this enormous
office that I had in DFID,

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:17.761
this tiny local by it's
in a concrete building.

00:28:17.761 --> 00:28:19.070
I was sitting with Rohini.

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:21.860
That morning, I had signed
off on, I think something

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:26.220
like $200 million for an
education program in Ethiopia.

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:27.940
And that afternoon I'm sitting

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:30.720
with Rohini now as the
Minister for Prisons, realizing

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:32.460
that we were gonna have to get rid

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:36.210
of the bottom five floors of
our office in central London

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:39.500
in order to try to save, I
think, nine or 10 million pounds

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:42.500
having just signed off on
200 million that morning.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:46.110
And this of course meant
that enormous problems

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:48.330
would then raise about
what on earth was happening

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:49.200
with this money, right?

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:51.640
When the public began looking at it.

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:53.200
And you can think of all
the kinds of problems

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:54.033
because we experienced them.

00:28:54.033 --> 00:28:56.720
And of course, this is
relevant for Joe Biden.

00:28:56.720 --> 00:28:58.060
This is relevant to Jake Sullivan.

00:28:58.060 --> 00:28:59.682
This is relevant for
Smith because of course

00:28:59.682 --> 00:29:02.730
what Jake Sullivan has been
writing about a great deal

00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:06.380
is the sense in which US foreign
policy cannot be detached

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:08.620
from what people, us citizens think.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:12.890
I mean, it's been for 60, 70 years

00:29:12.890 --> 00:29:14.640
really an elite occupation.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:17.172
People have tried to
remove foreign policy away

00:29:17.172 --> 00:29:19.233
from public opinion.

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:22.690
But in the end you have to justify it.

00:29:22.690 --> 00:29:25.380
You have to answer some
basic question, right?

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.920
So you say that you're
eliminating global poverty.

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:28.760
How are you doing?

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:30.440
You know, what have you been
doing it for the last 40 years?

00:29:30.440 --> 00:29:32.023
Where are your success stories?

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:35.490
You are working with this regime.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:37.380
So let's just run through some
of the problems in Britain.

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:38.940
And then I'll just flip
it back to human rights.

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:41.020
But what were the practical problems?

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:44.943
We were giving a hundred
million pounds a year, to India.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:48.040
Why, because India had
a lot of poor people

00:29:48.940 --> 00:29:50.710
but we then discovered

00:29:50.710 --> 00:29:52.970
that India was giving
exactly a hundred million

00:29:52.970 --> 00:29:55.463
pounds a year in development
aid to Afghanistan.

00:29:56.720 --> 00:29:58.360
Right, so the British
public has being asked

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:00.300
to give a a hundred million
pounds a year to India.

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:02.850
India is just giving all
that money to Afghanistan.

00:30:03.890 --> 00:30:06.990
Well, from the point of
view of DFID employees

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:08.170
there was no problem with this,

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:09.600
because there were people living

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:11.250
in extreme poverty in Northern India.

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:14.390
So they couldn't see why
anybody found this a problem.

00:30:14.390 --> 00:30:18.023
Or I would go into see
president Kagame in Rwanda.

00:30:18.879 --> 00:30:20.177
And I would talk about human rights

00:30:20.177 --> 00:30:21.890
the big human rights problems, in Rwanda.

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:23.530
And he would laugh and say

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:24.740
you don't care about human rights.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:25.670
And I'd say, what do you mean,

00:30:25.670 --> 00:30:26.630
I didn't care about human rights?

00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:29.630
And he'd say, "Quite explicitly
in this meeting, well

00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:32.080
you've just given us another
a hundred million pounds

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:32.913
of development aid.

00:30:32.913 --> 00:30:33.850
So if you cared about humans rights,

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:35.500
you wouldn't do that, would you?"

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:38.530
These conversations are
happening all the time.

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:41.180
That's before you get onto the
things that you can imagine.

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:43.570
Scandals about corruption,
scandals about waste,

00:30:43.570 --> 00:30:45.570
huge anxieties and bursts

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:47.470
which haven't yet hit the United States.

00:30:47.470 --> 00:30:48.679
But I promise you they are coming

00:30:48.679 --> 00:30:52.697
about the amounts of money
that people are spending

00:30:52.697 --> 00:30:56.210
and are paid running
development agencies, right?

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:58.280
Your average person in Britain,

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.870
who's being asked to give an on average

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.190
your average British citizen
would have had to give

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:04.230
if you look at the compare

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:06.020
the amount of money we were giving

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:10.440
to the population about $500
a year towards this, right?

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:12.610
So imagine maybe you've got to learn

00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:16.520
that you're giving an effect
2, $300 a year towards this.

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:18.660
It's very, very difficult for you

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:22.550
to understand why somebody
running a big charity

00:31:22.550 --> 00:31:24.490
is earning hundreds of
thousands of dollars a year.

00:31:24.490 --> 00:31:28.130
Because you think that this is being done

00:31:28.130 --> 00:31:31.310
as a sort of voluntary
charitable activity.

00:31:31.310 --> 00:31:32.670
You're imagining what's happening

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:35.920
in international development
in terms of, you know

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:37.750
what's happening in your local community.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:39.350
Somebody working on a very low income

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:41.530
out of a great sense of idealism.

00:31:41.530 --> 00:31:44.350
And suddenly you're having
to confront the fact

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:46.420
that these are organizations
with contractors

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:50.820
who are poverty experts
earning $1,500 a day

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:54.258
doing poverty alleviation in
somebody else's country, right?

00:31:54.258 --> 00:31:57.030
Now, let me just tie this
back to human rights.

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:59.242
So we can now get into
more of a conversation.

00:31:59.242 --> 00:32:02.780
Fundamentally, the question
around human rights is how

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:06.440
on earth do you as the United
States or the United Kingdom

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:10.280
or indeed anybody, develop
country strategies?

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:13.980
How do you decide whether
or not you are going to,

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:17.010
for example give money
to Rwanda and Ethiopia,

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:18.550
why do I choose them?

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:20.570
I choose them because they are

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:24.470
amongst the most brutal
authoritarian regimes in Africa.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:25.303
And they are also

00:32:25.303 --> 00:32:28.870
the greatest development success
stories in Africa, right?

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:30.960
They are the places that
development economists

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:32.271
are really excited by it.

00:32:32.271 --> 00:32:35.410
They're almost the only
two countries in Africa

00:32:35.410 --> 00:32:37.470
which people really get huge excited

00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:39.680
by because they've achieved
seven, 8%, even nine

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:43.043
10% growth over the last decade or so.

00:32:44.348 --> 00:32:46.830
And what sort of leverage do you have?

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:49.058
Because somewhere implicit

00:32:49.058 --> 00:32:53.060
in this conversation is
an idea that people had,

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:55.990
that you could improve the
human rights performance

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:58.959
of these countries by using the carrot

00:32:58.959 --> 00:33:01.763
of development aid as the way to do it.

00:33:02.597 --> 00:33:06.283
But in practice that almost never works.

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:09.810
And I saw it, and I'll finish
with these two examples.

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.930
I saw it in South Sudan where my colleague

00:33:12.930 --> 00:33:15.479
and friend Mark Green,
who was running USAID

00:33:15.479 --> 00:33:17.490
when I was running DFID,
went in to see Salva Kiir.

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:20.660
So horrible civil war
happening in South Sudan,

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:22.900
and the present of South Sudan Salva Kiir

00:33:22.900 --> 00:33:26.260
deeply implicated an
incredible brutal massacres

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:29.450
and horror and stealing
our development assistance.

00:33:29.450 --> 00:33:31.320
So extended food aid, and it's stolen.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:33.597
So the borders and
people being burnt down..

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:36.650
And Mark Green said, look

00:33:36.650 --> 00:33:39.370
if you continue behaving like this

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:41.380
we will cut off the development program

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:44.943
and Salva Kiir effectively
shrugged and said, fine.

00:33:44.943 --> 00:33:46.825
You know, you want to take the money away.

00:33:46.825 --> 00:33:47.658
That's your problem.

00:33:47.658 --> 00:33:48.830
If you want lots of poor people to start

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:50.826
that's your problem, take the money.

00:33:50.826 --> 00:33:51.659
I don't care.

00:33:51.659 --> 00:33:55.160
Right, and again, in Tanzania, Magufuli

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:57.530
president Magufuli who
the great anti COVID

00:33:57.530 --> 00:33:59.950
campaigner who's anti COVID campaign came

00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:01.895
to an unfortunate end a week ago

00:34:01.895 --> 00:34:06.130
refuse to take a call from the US state.

00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:10.080
The US ambassador refused
to go in and see him.

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:14.130
He declared the US ambassador
persona, kicked him out.

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:15.980
And the US government
was effectively forced

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:19.181
to come back again into
Tanzania a couple of years,

00:34:19.181 --> 00:34:20.014
basically with a billion dollars worth

00:34:20.014 --> 00:34:21.020
of development assistance

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:23.940
to try to buy their way
back into the good graces

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:26.810
of a relatively small country
in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:29.949
Because the assumptions that the US

00:34:29.949 --> 00:34:32.720
and the UK have about power,
that somehow they can use

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:34.250
even rather large sums of money

00:34:34.250 --> 00:34:36.340
as ways of changing the behavior of people

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:41.020
in these countries completely
underestimates the power

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:45.300
of nationalism state sovereignty,
the actual incentives

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:49.410
of people who are in political
power in countries ranging

00:34:49.410 --> 00:34:52.630
from Yanmar through to
Tanzania, and really calls

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:55.571
into question the extent to
which it is actually possible.

00:34:55.571 --> 00:34:57.640
Now, even if it would desirable actually

00:34:57.640 --> 00:34:59.920
possible to use development assistance

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:03.710
as a lever for transforming
attitudes to human rights.

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:05.550
Okay, enough for me.

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:07.030
- Thank you, Rory.

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:09.700
So Rory just talked
about some of the limits

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:13.040
of development aid as a
carrot, or in fact saying it

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:14.250
doesn't work.

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:16.090
Are there many examples of it not working?

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:19.020
So I'm curious before I ask
some questions, I haven't get

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:20.240
to questions from our audience.

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:22.470
Do any of our panelists
wanna react to that or react

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:25.430
to anything else you heard,
where you might be on the edge

00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:27.330
of your seat and have something to say?

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:28.940
Otherwise, we have some
great questions coming in

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:30.923
but I wanted to give
you all an opportunity.

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:37.670
Okay, so I'm gonna go
ahead and ask a question

00:35:37.670 --> 00:35:41.331
that Sam posed or turn it into a question.

00:35:41.331 --> 00:35:44.370
Sam basically said, the
question we're asking today,

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:47.281
are human rights back on the
international aid agenda?

00:35:47.281 --> 00:35:49.683
Might not be the framing he would use

00:35:49.683 --> 00:35:51.530
and that he's not quite

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:54.310
sure we're really on the
brink of major change.

00:35:54.310 --> 00:35:56.000
I'm curious, what do our
other panelists think?

00:35:56.000 --> 00:36:00.830
Is this a critical turning
point or not necessarily?

00:36:00.830 --> 00:36:03.000
- Let me throw a second

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:04.530
and then come to people.

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:07.000
It seems to me that part of
this needs to be understood

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:10.190
in terms of competition with China.

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:13.210
And in particular in attempt by the Biden

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:17.840
and Johnson and others
to try to frame democracy

00:36:17.840 --> 00:36:19.990
as an alternative to
authoritarian regimes.

00:36:19.990 --> 00:36:23.520
And so in so far as human
rights gets back onto the agenda

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:25.160
I suspect a lot of it will be

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:28.570
through the prison of
trying to create a coalition

00:36:29.670 --> 00:36:31.273
of inadvertent comments democracies,

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:37.350
in this new form of competitional
conflict with China.

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:39.480
But again, and I think I'd love to hear

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:41.190
from Melissa on this,

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:43.830
the experience of the cold war suggests

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:48.240
that although you may talk
about creating democracies

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:50.750
as the alternative to
your authoritarian rivals,

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:53.222
in practice you end up with cooperating

00:36:53.222 --> 00:36:54.767
with people who aren't democracies at all.

00:36:57.480 --> 00:36:58.313
- I'm glad we're returning

00:36:58.313 --> 00:37:00.670
to the topic of China,
thoughts on that, Melissa?

00:37:02.310 --> 00:37:07.310
- Yeah I mean, I think that
it's sort of a matter of degree

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:11.950
in terms of like what you're
doing and what you can achieve.

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:14.891
And so I, you know, I agree
that there is no, you know, like

00:37:14.891 --> 00:37:19.100
whether you're coming at it
from the side of kind of the US

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:21.670
or the Soviet union during
the cold war, there's not

00:37:21.670 --> 00:37:24.350
like neither kind of group
has a great track record

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:28.399
of being able to essentially
like force countries

00:37:28.399 --> 00:37:30.240
to take on their model.

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:32.750
Whether you look at the
Soviet Union in Afghanistan,

00:37:32.750 --> 00:37:35.010
the US in Vietnam, you know

00:37:35.010 --> 00:37:37.350
there's maybe some success stories

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:40.341
like Parma comes to mind
from the perspective

00:37:40.341 --> 00:37:42.520
of the US but I think it just sort of like

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:45.950
it depends on what you wanna achieve.

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:48.960
And I agree that if the
goal was to, you know,

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:52.730
you have some brutal
dictator and you think

00:37:52.730 --> 00:37:55.720
that with the carrot
of this development aid

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:56.570
you're gonna change his policies.

00:37:56.570 --> 00:37:58.030
Like that's just not realistic

00:37:58.030 --> 00:38:00.209
and that's just not going to happen.

00:38:00.209 --> 00:38:02.630
But, you know, there
have been like, you know

00:38:02.630 --> 00:38:06.790
a series of studies looking,
you know, in conflict zones

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:10.720
and Afghanistan or Pakistan,
the Philippines looking

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:12.850
at the role of the
development aid programs.

00:38:12.850 --> 00:38:14.650
And I'd say the literature is kind of

00:38:14.650 --> 00:38:16.770
like a little bit mixed, you know

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:21.040
but there's some evidence
that these programs

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:22.960
at least it's gonna depend on the details

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:25.470
but there can be success
in terms of giving people

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:28.280
like an alternative besides
supporting the Taliban.

00:38:28.280 --> 00:38:30.410
So I think that when you
think about things on kind of

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:35.410
like a more granular level,
as opposed to, you know

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:40.100
looking at political change
at the national level

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:44.291
but then there can be
kind of more examples

00:38:44.291 --> 00:38:47.753
where things come sort
of closer to achieving

00:38:50.149 --> 00:38:52.090
what the objectives are.

00:38:52.090 --> 00:38:54.661
But I agree that I don't
think we really know

00:38:54.661 --> 00:38:55.950
how this works.

00:38:55.950 --> 00:38:57.701
We understand a lot more

00:38:57.701 --> 00:39:00.460
about what you would
need to do when a world

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:03.210
without politics to alleviate poverty,

00:39:03.210 --> 00:39:05.900
then how you actually do that

00:39:05.900 --> 00:39:07.910
in a political context
where there's people

00:39:07.910 --> 00:39:10.821
who just don't care, actually
benefit from the fact,

00:39:10.821 --> 00:39:15.023
that lots of people are living in poverty.

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:19.000
- Thanks, Melissa, and well
put in terms of how this works

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:21.310
in a world without politics
versus a world with politics

00:39:21.310 --> 00:39:22.180
which is part of the value

00:39:22.180 --> 00:39:25.190
in bringing academics and
practitioners together.

00:39:25.190 --> 00:39:27.300
Sam, I see you wanted to jump in.

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:28.410
- It's so interesting.

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:29.400
I just, you know,

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:31.760
I'm very averse to the general framing

00:39:31.760 --> 00:39:35.550
