00:43:42.733 --> 00:43:46.450
Given the lots of interests are leading us

00:43:46.450 --> 00:43:48.200
towards this new cold war framing.

00:43:48.200 --> 00:43:52.320
- Let me just jump in
with one question I think

00:43:52.320 --> 00:43:54.620
which is on moving from
thinking about bi laterals

00:43:54.620 --> 00:43:56.720
to thinking about
multilateral institutions.

00:43:56.720 --> 00:43:59.890
And maybe the, when we
think about the human rights

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:01.010
perhaps the one to really bring

00:44:01.010 --> 00:44:03.793
on stage is to think
about the United Nations.

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:07.240
I think in the 1990s

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:11.490
what we did see was the UN
played quite an important role

00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:15.730
in terms of human rights
by entering the stage

00:44:15.730 --> 00:44:17.970
when a newly independent countries coming

00:44:17.970 --> 00:44:20.360
out of conflict for writing constitutions.

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:22.610
So we saw a lot of UN push

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:24.210
that the one that I'm the most familiar

00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:25.890
with is on gender quarters.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:27.510
So I think in country, after country

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:30.580
the UN pushed very hard
to have gender quotas put

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:32.720
in place instance in Rwanda.

00:44:32.720 --> 00:44:35.387
And it was accepted by the
countries at that point in time

00:44:35.387 --> 00:44:37.960
because very often women
will say less implicated

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:39.100
in conflict.

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:43.580
And so they were more willing
to have them be part of it.

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:46.350
And so, you know, certainly
one reason why in Rwanda now

00:44:46.350 --> 00:44:51.350
it's a majority female parliament,
and we can debate whether

00:44:51.590 --> 00:44:54.090
or not had it had a role,
but that that's one place

00:44:54.090 --> 00:44:57.040
where I think the UN, which
in many other places, you know

00:44:57.040 --> 00:45:00.000
doesn't have much money
or much influence was able

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:03.220
to strategically make a
difference by entering

00:45:03.220 --> 00:45:05.120
on constitution writing.

00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:07.220
So I'd be curious if we sort of see places

00:45:08.259 --> 00:45:10.150
where perhaps maybe less bi laterals,

00:45:10.150 --> 00:45:12.580
but more multi laterals could enter.

00:45:12.580 --> 00:45:15.080
I think the flip side of
the UN is the World Bank,

00:45:16.179 --> 00:45:17.509
which for the longest time said

00:45:17.509 --> 00:45:18.630
it would not engage with
politics or governance.

00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:21.600
It was very explicit about
it, but it seems to have more

00:45:23.179 --> 00:45:25.078
recently been willing to start
thinking about it perhaps

00:45:25.078 --> 00:45:27.528
through a more anodyne
term, like state capacity.

00:45:29.538 --> 00:45:33.400
- Very quickly on that one.

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:35.989
But I mean, I think the,
you are actually right.

00:45:35.989 --> 00:45:37.717
There's something very interesting

00:45:37.717 --> 00:45:39.090
that you've pointed out that in Rwanda,

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:40.160
because this a paradox, isn't that?

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:41.760
On the one hand they've achieved

00:45:42.899 --> 00:45:44.297
these remarkables statistics

00:45:44.297 --> 00:45:46.470
and it was a female
participation parliament.

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:48.210
On the other hand, it is an intense

00:45:48.210 --> 00:45:52.360
the authoritarian regime, which
has included assassination

00:45:52.360 --> 00:45:54.640
of political opponents and
all forms of oppression.

00:45:54.640 --> 00:45:59.000
So I think this may be
rather interesting theme

00:45:59.000 --> 00:46:02.700
that could be pursued if we
had more time about the ways

00:46:02.700 --> 00:46:07.700
in which conversations about
diversity and conversations

00:46:09.053 --> 00:46:11.230
about what we traditionally called liberty

00:46:11.230 --> 00:46:13.163
maybe heading in different directions.

00:46:15.041 --> 00:46:15.901
In other words

00:46:15.901 --> 00:46:18.293
that it would be possible
to create a regime.

00:46:19.372 --> 00:46:21.213
And indeed I think that
beginning to emerge

00:46:21.213 --> 00:46:24.390
which seemed to resemble
our images of diversity

00:46:28.110 --> 00:46:32.137
without bringing the kinds of
benefits in terms of liberty

00:46:32.137 --> 00:46:33.630
and maybe ultimately in terms

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:36.173
of stability and peace that
you would have anticipated.

00:46:37.862 --> 00:46:41.209
- I'll just add on to that,
you know, I think Rohini

00:46:41.209 --> 00:46:43.709
has asked a really important
question, but there's

00:46:46.171 --> 00:46:50.400
in a way history to this present
divergence that Rory notes.

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:55.170
I mean, just to cite one example,
the authoritarian feminism

00:46:55.170 --> 00:46:59.850
of someone like Princess
Ashraf of Iran of Iran

00:47:00.923 --> 00:47:03.980
before her brother's
government was toppled,

00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:07.530
which the Western power
supported of course.

00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:11.990
And that was another example
where there, there was a lot

00:47:11.990 --> 00:47:14.318
of talk about the regimes feminism

00:47:14.318 --> 00:47:18.364
and she in particular
talked about a lot about it

00:47:18.364 --> 00:47:22.470
at global UN human rights conferences,

00:47:22.470 --> 00:47:26.863
the world over notably in the
Tyronn conference at in 1968.

00:47:30.618 --> 00:47:32.790
But of course that didn't,
that the Iranian regime

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:36.200
was bringing a liberty generally.

00:47:36.200 --> 00:47:38.853
And of course it
eventually paid the price.

