in the grand scheme of
things fairly easily,

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:33.220
like, you know, development aid.

00:51:33.220 --> 00:51:36.100
So I'm certainly not saying
to abandon development

00:51:36.100 --> 00:51:39.598
assistance or, you know,
the like these objectives

00:51:39.598 --> 00:51:42.920
to have like, you know state
capacity as Rohini said

00:51:42.920 --> 00:51:45.190
which is kind of how it gets
framed, like in the context

00:51:45.190 --> 00:51:48.213
of Central America or, you
know, development assistance.

00:51:49.699 --> 00:51:50.532
But I think that, you know,
like as we've talked about

00:51:50.532 --> 00:51:52.150
they don't have a great
track record and maybe

00:51:52.150 --> 00:51:54.700
like another perspective
on it is that by, you know

00:51:54.700 --> 00:51:57.287
fixing some of these problems at home,

00:51:57.287 --> 00:52:00.720
we're less likely to just
have this collateral damage

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:03.510
or go and be, you know,
propping up dictators

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:06.300
during these other things
that have horrible effects.

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:08.390
- Sam, I see you wanted
to jump in quickly.

00:52:08.390 --> 00:52:11.070
- Yeah, no, I think the
question is really well taken

00:52:11.070 --> 00:52:14.450
because apart from the
let's say difficult optics

00:52:14.450 --> 00:52:19.000
that Joe Biden faces to the
extent he has to, you know

00:52:19.840 --> 00:52:23.500
have exclusionary politics at the border

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:27.280
of any kind he has, you know

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:30.480
and liberals generally have
a difficulty of figuring

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:33.140
out how to connect the
short-term and the long-term.

00:52:33.140 --> 00:52:36.200
So in the short term with
folks at the border border

00:52:36.200 --> 00:52:38.510
there is a human rights
obligation minimally

00:52:38.510 --> 00:52:42.697
to hear and grant, you know,
legitimate asylum claims

00:52:42.697 --> 00:52:47.697
even when migrants are economic, migrants

00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:50.660
and converting their claims into the kind

00:52:50.660 --> 00:52:53.540
of claims that can be taken seriously.

00:52:53.540 --> 00:52:56.937
But in the longterm,
it's not clear to me that

00:52:56.937 --> 00:52:59.555
the global anti-poverty
agenda is ever going to

00:52:59.555 --> 00:53:04.555
by itself alleviate the
drivers of migration.

00:53:05.046 --> 00:53:06.470
And this is an opportunity
for me to introduce

00:53:06.470 --> 00:53:08.777
like the main theme of my
recent book, which is a contrast

00:53:08.777 --> 00:53:12.750
between these ideas about
sufficient provision

00:53:12.750 --> 00:53:16.723
whether generous or ungenerous
and this very different idea

00:53:16.723 --> 00:53:20.560
of a more egalitarian living standards.

00:53:20.560 --> 00:53:24.390
It seems like dubious to
me that once a peasant gets

00:53:24.390 --> 00:53:26.894
above a dollar in change a
day, he'll decide not to move

00:53:26.894 --> 00:53:30.540
to the United States if he can get there.

00:53:30.540 --> 00:53:32.930
And so I totally agree

00:53:32.930 --> 00:53:37.050
with Melissa about the
looming climate refugee crisis

00:53:37.050 --> 00:53:41.020
but quite apart from it, if
we don't focus our development

00:53:41.020 --> 00:53:43.940
thinking on greater equalization

00:53:43.940 --> 00:53:45.930
over the long-term of standards of living,

00:53:45.930 --> 00:53:49.270
we will have economic migration
for as long as we can see.

00:53:49.270 --> 00:53:52.100
And then the attendant
border politics that you know

00:53:52.100 --> 00:53:54.573
are cross Mediterranean
politics that we've seen

00:53:54.573 --> 00:53:58.290
everywhere in the world in recent decades.

00:53:58.290 --> 00:54:00.090
- I wanna bring in one more question

00:54:01.137 --> 00:54:02.910
and it has to do with China

00:54:02.910 --> 00:54:04.110
and I was hoping to bring it in earlier

00:54:04.110 --> 00:54:06.110
but I think I'd like to broaden the frame

00:54:06.110 --> 00:54:08.160
as Rohini did earlier thinking, not just

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:10.723
in terms of bi laterals,
but multi laterals

00:54:10.723 --> 00:54:14.670
and we'll have a quick go
around on this question.

00:54:14.670 --> 00:54:16.450
And then I wanna have
time for closing thoughts

00:54:16.450 --> 00:54:21.160
but Thomas Kingston asks, "Having
worked in human rights law

00:54:21.160 --> 00:54:23.900
in Cambodia, and having
witnessed their governments shift

00:54:23.900 --> 00:54:28.073
from a pro European pro US
stance to a pro China stance.

00:54:29.465 --> 00:54:30.720
It seems to me that there's a limitation

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:33.500
of how human rights promotion
can be achieved through aid

00:54:33.500 --> 00:54:35.360
especially in less democratic systems.

00:54:35.360 --> 00:54:38.370
When China is offering support
that comes without moral

00:54:38.370 --> 00:54:40.714
or democratic obligations,
how can this be overcome

00:54:40.714 --> 00:54:43.490
or is it just going to
result in a bidding war?

00:54:43.490 --> 00:54:46.220
So I think this builds on some
of what we discussed earlier

00:54:46.220 --> 00:54:48.460
but what I appreciate
about this question is it's

00:54:48.460 --> 00:54:52.650
from the perspective of
countries receiving assistance.

00:54:52.650 --> 00:54:55.180
And I think you see this
playing out in many countries

00:54:55.180 --> 00:54:58.210
in Africa as well, receiving
assistance from China

00:54:58.210 --> 00:55:00.780
but any quick thoughts in
response to that question?

00:55:00.780 --> 00:55:01.613
'Cause I wanted to bring

00:55:01.613 --> 00:55:03.763
in a perspective from
the audience on China.

00:55:04.710 --> 00:55:07.420
- So very quickly, I think the big change

00:55:07.420 --> 00:55:11.750
since the UN intervention in Cambodia

00:55:11.750 --> 00:55:15.190
is the incredible shift in
the sort of status prestige,

00:55:15.190 --> 00:55:17.290
power and weight of China

00:55:17.290 --> 00:55:19.250
is the big factor over the last 20 years.

00:55:19.250 --> 00:55:21.100
I mean, from a British point of view

00:55:21.100 --> 00:55:24.330
one way of thinking
about it is that in 2005

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:27.900
the British economy was larger
than the Chinese economy,

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:30.520
and the Chinese economy
is now seven times larger

00:55:31.456 --> 00:55:32.289
than the British economy.

00:55:32.289 --> 00:55:33.780
So that's happened just in 15 years,

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:38.780
the same relationship has
emerged throughout the world.

00:55:38.900 --> 00:55:41.600
And therefore it doesn't
matter whether you're talking

00:55:44.725 --> 00:55:47.335
about Cambodia or whether
you're talking about Zimbabwe,

00:55:47.335 --> 00:55:50.260
the basic story of the wait

00:55:50.260 --> 00:55:52.680
and procedure of the United States

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:57.430
and its allies seem to have
in the 1990s has evaporated

00:55:57.430 --> 00:55:59.580
and being replaced by
that status of China.

00:56:02.560 --> 00:56:04.280
- Thanks Rory, Sam I see
you wanna jump in there?

00:56:04.280 --> 00:56:05.550
- Yeah, just briefly.

00:56:05.550 --> 00:56:07.210
It's we shouldn't forget

00:56:07.210 --> 00:56:10.190
that the geopolitical
competition is sometimes

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:12.490
a great boon to the world's peoples

00:56:12.490 --> 00:56:16.130
especially when they can
play competing hedgemonts

00:56:16.130 --> 00:56:17.300
off against each other.

00:56:17.300 --> 00:56:19.160
So Melissa's story is great

00:56:19.160 --> 00:56:22.770
but we shouldn't think the
alternative is, you know, force

00:56:22.770 --> 00:56:26.950
and hearts and minds, which in
any way was never unconnected

00:56:26.950 --> 00:56:28.750
from force in the Vietnam case

00:56:28.750 --> 00:56:31.750
like the Phoenix program
and things like that.

00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:34.610
But the broader story
of cold war development

00:56:34.610 --> 00:56:36.860
is that there are a lot of canny actors

00:56:36.860 --> 00:56:38.650
across the global South who understood

00:56:38.650 --> 00:56:42.470
that they could play rivals
off against each other

00:56:42.470 --> 00:56:44.140
to get more from each.

00:56:44.140 --> 00:56:46.610
A great example of that
as in David Angerman

00:56:46.610 --> 00:56:48.580
our colleague's recent
book "The Price of Aid"

00:56:48.580 --> 00:56:52.560
about how Indian states men
basically planners and others

00:56:52.560 --> 00:56:53.760
were able to take a lot

00:56:53.760 --> 00:56:56.696
from both Americans and the
Soviet Union, because they knew

00:56:56.696 --> 00:57:01.696
that their generosity
was not just innocent

00:57:01.775 --> 00:57:06.775
but reflected these competing,
competition ideologies.

00:57:08.640 --> 00:57:10.540
- Sam, actually just
mentioned one of our panelists

00:57:10.540 --> 00:57:13.130
from the first of our Yale
Development Dialogue Series.

00:57:13.130 --> 00:57:14.278
So it brings it full circle.

00:57:14.278 --> 00:57:16.030
We only have a couple minutes left

00:57:16.030 --> 00:57:17.220
and I wanna make sure to go back

00:57:17.220 --> 00:57:18.856
to each of you with any closing thoughts.

00:57:18.856 --> 00:57:22.310
Earlier, I think it was
Melissa who said we're not

00:57:22.310 --> 00:57:26.310
being pessimistic just
realistic, but I don't know

00:57:26.310 --> 00:57:28.870
if there's a chance to
end on a note of optimism.

00:57:28.870 --> 00:57:30.610
I think what I'd love to hear

00:57:30.610 --> 00:57:34.053
from each of you is kind
of back to where we began.

00:57:35.518 --> 00:57:36.380
Why this question, why now?

00:57:36.380 --> 00:57:38.170
And what do you hope to see?

00:57:38.170 --> 00:57:41.863
So anything more forward-looking,
who wants to jump in?

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:44.030
Rory.

00:57:44.030 --> 00:57:47.170
- Okay, I mean, I'd say
just closing thoughts.

00:57:47.170 --> 00:57:48.240
First thing is that at the core

00:57:48.240 --> 00:57:49.950
of a lot of what we're talking about

00:57:49.950 --> 00:57:52.410
is basically modernization theory.

00:57:52.410 --> 00:57:53.623
This idea from the 1950s

00:57:53.623 --> 00:57:54.991
that there is somehow
some natural relationship

00:57:54.991 --> 00:57:59.991
between being prosperous or
being free and being democratic.

00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:06.170
And we are struggling now,
partly because of China, partly

00:58:07.680 --> 00:58:09.770
because of populism with the possibility

00:58:09.770 --> 00:58:11.850
that those things don't
go as naturally together,

00:58:11.850 --> 00:58:13.210
as we assumed.

00:58:13.210 --> 00:58:16.747
However, I would say, if you
want me to end with a note

00:58:16.747 --> 00:58:17.580
of optimism and this made me touch some,

00:58:17.580 --> 00:58:19.320
let's just say I have seen a lot

00:58:19.320 --> 00:58:23.040
of very good small scale
programs on support human rights.

00:58:23.040 --> 00:58:25.350
And I feel, in Pakistan
and Bangladesh, for example

00:58:25.350 --> 00:58:26.600
that small amounts

00:58:27.742 --> 00:58:29.803
of money supporting civil
society organizations, some very

00:58:29.803 --> 00:58:31.710
very courageous civil
society organizations

00:58:31.710 --> 00:58:33.090
has been extraordinary and
the counterfactual, you know

00:58:33.090 --> 00:58:38.090
what would happen if there had
been no support whatsoever?

00:58:39.070 --> 00:58:42.712
The human rights campaign is
working on minority communities

00:58:42.712 --> 00:58:44.640
in Pakistan, I think is
very, very worrying indeed.

00:58:44.640 --> 00:58:47.310
So I think it's a very good thing

00:58:47.310 --> 00:58:48.580
for development agencies to do.

00:58:48.580 --> 00:58:50.560
I think it's always gonna
be a very small part

00:58:50.560 --> 00:58:51.430
of their budget.

00:58:51.430 --> 00:58:53.100
In fact, if what you're talking about

00:58:53.100 --> 00:58:55.580
is supporting activists on the ground

00:58:55.580 --> 00:58:58.187
that's not gonna remotely
compare with the, you know

00:58:58.187 --> 00:59:01.700
$20 billion that you're
putting into the other stuff.

00:59:01.700 --> 00:59:03.997
But I think there is
still scope to do a lot.

00:59:03.997 --> 00:59:08.470
And I think we can continue to
do it when conditions allow.

00:59:08.470 --> 00:59:09.303
- Thanks, Rory.

00:59:09.303 --> 00:59:11.340
I want to close with Rohini
but first Sam or Melissa,

00:59:11.340 --> 00:59:13.610
do either of you wanna jump
in with any final thoughts

00:59:13.610 --> 00:59:15.290
in our, we're at time,

00:59:15.290 --> 00:59:17.270
but I wanna make sure to bring you in,

00:59:17.270 --> 00:59:18.380
if you have something else.

00:59:18.380 --> 00:59:20.811
- I completely agree
with what was just said.

00:59:20.811 --> 00:59:23.643
So oftentimes in this,
in like development,

00:59:23.643 --> 00:59:26.550
there's this big debate
between like, Oh, doing these,

00:59:26.550 --> 00:59:29.923
like, you know big picture
things that don't like, you know

00:59:29.923 --> 00:59:33.083
like make a dramatic
difference, you know JP

00:59:33.083 --> 00:59:36.483
and like on a big picture versus
the smaller scale programs

00:59:36.483 --> 00:59:41.483
where it's just easier to see
success because the number

00:59:41.890 --> 00:59:44.820
of things that need to change
to make a dramatic difference

00:59:44.820 --> 00:59:46.620
in kind of levels of
poverty are just huge.

00:59:46.620 --> 00:59:47.990
And we don't really have an understanding

00:59:47.990 --> 00:59:50.510
of how politics works
or the economics works.

00:59:50.510 --> 00:59:52.840
And so it's not, we should
be asking those questions.

00:59:52.840 --> 00:59:56.080
I think they're important
questions, but it's easier to see

00:59:56.080 --> 00:59:59.100
and actually to evaluate
the successes when you look

00:59:59.100 --> 01:00:01.400
at some of the smaller scale programs.

01:00:01.400 --> 01:00:02.320
So I agree that like

01:00:02.320 --> 01:00:03.910
for political reasons that some are gonna

01:00:03.910 --> 01:00:07.200
be the main thing, but
that, that should be kind

01:00:07.200 --> 01:00:09.830
of a central central
thing moving forward, just

01:00:09.830 --> 01:00:12.990
because we have more hopes
when we're making small changes

01:00:12.990 --> 01:00:14.470
and are trying to make small changes

01:00:14.470 --> 01:00:18.330
that you know, that we're
gonna see effectiveness there.

01:00:18.330 --> 01:00:19.980
And so I think that, you know, in addition

01:00:19.980 --> 01:00:22.600
to having these kind of
ambitious talks about how

01:00:22.600 --> 01:00:26.023
is everything changing and
it's a new paradigm, you know

01:00:26.023 --> 01:00:28.230
just thinking about the
things that have worked,

01:00:28.230 --> 01:00:30.323
even if they're small scale and, you know,

01:00:30.323 --> 01:00:33.470
doing what we can to advocate for those.

01:00:33.470 --> 01:00:36.500
And as I said, also to, you
know, thinking about, you know

01:00:36.500 --> 01:00:39.100
trying to have more
appreciation for how, you know

01:00:39.100 --> 01:00:41.930
there's policies that can actually benefit

01:00:41.930 --> 01:00:45.864
the home countries that are
supporting this aid benefit

01:00:45.864 --> 01:00:47.120
you know, the median voter

01:00:47.120 --> 01:00:52.120
and also benefit people
elsewhere in the world.

01:00:54.744 --> 01:00:55.812
- Thanks Melissa.

01:00:55.812 --> 01:00:57.064
Sam, final thoughts from you?

01:00:57.064 --> 01:00:58.730
- Sure, well, you know, there's
always ground for optimism.

01:00:58.730 --> 01:01:01.110
They have to involve
seizing opportunities.

01:01:01.110 --> 01:01:03.784
And I'd go back to the very beginning

01:01:03.784 --> 01:01:08.784
with Rohini and Rory about
the opportunity that is open

01:01:09.320 --> 01:01:13.544
to us now because elites in
wealthy countries are frightened

01:01:13.544 --> 01:01:18.330
and they they've been, they
know they've been thrown out

01:01:18.330 --> 01:01:21.890
of power in some cases,
and they're eager to figure

01:01:21.890 --> 01:01:25.130
out what that means and change their ways.

01:01:25.130 --> 01:01:27.720
Jake Sullivan is a great
example who remarked

01:01:27.720 --> 01:01:31.334
in 2017 that he had the
humility of the defeated.

01:01:31.334 --> 01:01:36.072
And I think like Lincoln,
Sullivan others are evolving

01:01:36.072 --> 01:01:38.134
in really interesting ways.

01:01:38.134 --> 01:01:40.440
The question is what they do next.

01:01:40.440 --> 01:01:43.560
And I think we should
warn them against a repeat

01:01:43.560 --> 01:01:48.560
of the cold war for some of
the reasons Melissa mentioned

01:01:49.212 --> 01:01:52.881
but also because it doesn't
even seem to make any sense

01:01:52.881 --> 01:01:57.881
with the new adversary
and instead push them

01:01:58.380 --> 01:02:02.570
in ways that will make the
world's people's better off

01:02:02.570 --> 01:02:05.553
in a series of ways.

01:02:07.080 --> 01:02:08.630
- Rohini, back to you to close.

01:02:08.630 --> 01:02:11.870
I thank you for putting
this topic on our agenda

01:02:11.870 --> 01:02:12.703
for the series.

01:02:12.703 --> 01:02:13.536
I think it's been fascinating.

01:02:13.536 --> 01:02:15.163
What are your final thoughts?

01:02:15.163 --> 01:02:17.820
- I think I would just echo
what all the panelists say

01:02:17.820 --> 01:02:20.300
maybe the one thing I
would add, which is a bit

01:02:20.300 --> 01:02:23.530
and we're looking for the US
that just given the huge role

01:02:23.530 --> 01:02:26.520
that I would say tech
giants are playing now,

01:02:26.520 --> 01:02:28.900
a lot what the US is going to decide to do

01:02:28.900 --> 01:02:33.900
in terms of antitrust, in
terms of how to, you know,

01:02:34.000 --> 01:02:37.620
get them to think about uses of data

01:02:37.620 --> 01:02:39.210
of when it's going to be
used for surveillance.

01:02:39.210 --> 01:02:40.940
When it's going to be is for transparency.

01:02:40.940 --> 01:02:42.750
And it's gonna be incredibly important

01:02:42.750 --> 01:02:46.330
in a lot of low-income
countries, but also in countries

01:02:46.330 --> 01:02:50.120
like Hong Kong, China,
other places where, you know

01:02:50.120 --> 01:02:53.050
what we are seeing is that
ability to, for civil society

01:02:53.050 --> 01:02:55.400
and others to protest,
to push for human rights

01:02:57.574 --> 01:02:58.837
depends a lot on how they are being able

01:02:58.837 --> 01:03:01.253
to use social media right now
for coordinating protests.

01:03:02.910 --> 01:03:03.743
- Thank you, Rohini.

01:03:03.743 --> 01:03:04.730
Well, I know we're over time.

01:03:04.730 --> 01:03:06.420
Thank you all for bearing with us.

01:03:06.420 --> 01:03:07.480
I am really glad though

01:03:07.480 --> 01:03:08.890
that we were able to go back and hear

01:03:08.890 --> 01:03:11.576
from each of our panelists
on this very important topic.

01:03:11.576 --> 01:03:14.350
And I think we were emerging

01:03:14.350 --> 01:03:16.230
with more questions
that need to be answered

01:03:16.230 --> 01:03:17.874
but we'll keep the conversation going.

01:03:17.874 --> 01:03:22.650
We look forward to seeing all
of you in our next iteration

01:03:22.650 --> 01:03:25.150
of the Yale Development
Dialogue Series, where again

01:03:25.150 --> 01:03:27.670
we're bringing together
historians and practitioners

01:03:27.670 --> 01:03:30.329
for conversations on these complex topics

01:03:30.329 --> 01:03:32.610
as they affect low and
middle income countries.

01:03:32.610 --> 01:03:34.329
I think this was a great example

01:03:34.329 --> 01:03:36.370
of why there's such value in that framing.

01:03:36.370 --> 01:03:37.859
So thank you to all of our panelists,

01:03:37.859 --> 01:03:40.659
thank you to our attendees,
and we'll see you next time.

